WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.627
Our store keeps every payment in one file, and it only ever adds to the end.

2
00:00:07.627 --> 00:00:11.192
Payment 1 arrives, PENDING, still waiting for the bank.

3
00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:12.165
Then payment 2.

4
00:00:12.165 --> 00:00:18.193
Then the money for payment 1 really moves, and its new status, CAPTURED, goes at the end too.

5
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:22.998
Each line is one record, and auth:1 is its key, the name we look it up by.

6
00:00:22.998 --> 00:00:30.007
The numbers on the left are byte offsets: where each record begins, counted in bytes from the start of the file.

7
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:33.845
Now the card reader asks: what's the status of auth:1?

8
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.403
The simplest answer is a scan.

9
00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:41.175
Read from the start, and keep the last value you see for that key.

10
00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:44.357
PENDING at byte 0, then CAPTURED at byte 66.

11
00:00:44.357 --> 00:00:45.658
The last one wins.

12
00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:48.261
That cost all 100 bytes of the file.

13
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:55.157
With a million records of about 40 bytes, every lookup reads 40 megabytes, and it grows with every payment.

14
00:00:55.157 --> 00:00:59.241
That's rereading the shop's whole history to answer one question.

15
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.631
We want to jump straight to auth:1's newest value, and read only that.

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.296
The obvious idea: give each key a fixed spot, and write over it.

17
00:01:08.296 --> 00:01:10.243
Nothing old, nothing to scan.

18
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.172
Let's be fair and try it.

19
00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.745
auth:1's spot holds PENDING, 7 bytes.

20
00:01:14.745 --> 00:01:18.570
CAPTURED is 8, so its last byte lands on auth:2's spot.

21
00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.535
Make the spots bigger, and every short value wastes room.

22
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:25.884
Worse, cut the power in the middle of the write.

23
00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:28.115
C, A, P, T, and the lights go out.

24
00:01:28.115 --> 00:01:32.644
The spot now says CAPTING: half new, half old, and no copy of either.

25
00:01:32.644 --> 00:01:34.941
Not a status any bank has heard of.

26
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.812
So writing over old bytes is the risky part, and our file never does it.

27
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.628
A café's paper notebook shows the way.

28
00:01:42.628 --> 00:01:48.262
The owner writes each sale on the next empty line, in pen, and never rubs anything out.

29
00:01:48.262 --> 00:01:50.917
A correction is a new line at the bottom.

30
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:57.097
At the front, she keeps a slip of paper that says which line holds each customer's latest entry.

31
00:01:57.097 --> 00:02:01.033
To look someone up, she reads the slip, then turns to one line.

32
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.034
Let's map it.

33
00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:05.369
The notebook is our file, and each line is a record.

34
00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:07.742
Where a line sits is its byte offset.

35
00:02:07.742 --> 00:02:13.259
And the slip is a small map in memory, from each key to where its newest value starts.

36
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.112
Let's write the slip.

37
00:02:15.112 --> 00:02:22.103
Every record starts with a 20-byte header that holds its sizes, then the key, then the value.

38
00:02:22.103 --> 00:02:25.786
auth:2's record is at 33, and its key is 6 bytes.

39
00:02:25.786 --> 00:02:29.544
So its value starts at 33 plus 20 plus 6: byte 59.

40
00:02:29.544 --> 00:02:31.273
And PENDING is 7 bytes.

41
00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.130
Now auth:1 has two records.

42
00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.656
Which one should the slip point at, and at which byte?

43
00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.701
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:42.351
The newest, at 66.

45
00:02:42.351 --> 00:02:46.288
Plus 20, plus 6: byte 92, and CAPTURED is 8 bytes.

46
00:02:46.288 --> 00:02:51.957
The PENDING record at 0 stays in the file, but nothing points at it now.

47
00:02:51.957 --> 00:02:53.611
Those are dead bytes.

48
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.818
Now ask again: get auth:1.

49
00:02:55.818 --> 00:02:58.105
The slip says byte 92, 8 bytes.

50
00:02:58.105 --> 00:03:01.351
One read of exactly those 8 bytes: CAPTURED.

51
00:03:01.351 --> 00:03:04.892
Eight bytes, where the scan read the whole file.

52
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.962
And a key that isn't on the slip, like auth:7?

53
00:03:07.962 --> 00:03:09.765
No trip to the drive at all.

54
00:03:09.765 --> 00:03:11.761
The cheapest question there is.

55
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.091
Now payment 2 is captured.

56
00:03:14.091 --> 00:03:21.134
The store adds a record at the end, at 100, and moves auth:2's line on the slip to byte 126.

57
00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:23.354
The record at 33 is dead too.

58
00:03:23.354 --> 00:03:25.268
Nothing was written over.

59
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.755
And a delete can't rub anything out either.

60
00:03:28.755 --> 00:03:36.013
Deleting auth:1 adds a tombstone at 134: the key, with no value, and a value size of minus 1.

61
00:03:36.013 --> 00:03:37.729
The slip drops auth:1.

62
00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:45.390
This design is Bitcask, a storage engine that the company Basho built in 2010 for its database, Riak.

63
00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.653
A file you only add to is an append-only log.

64
00:03:48.653 --> 00:03:56.049
And the slip is the keydir, the store's index: a lookup table from each key to where its record lives.

65
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.253
Here the café picture breaks.

66
00:03:58.253 --> 00:04:06.113
Paper survives the night, but the keydir lives in RAM, the computer's memory, which forgets everything when the power goes.

67
00:04:06.113 --> 00:04:11.097
So after a crash, the store rebuilds it by reading the whole file from byte 0.

68
00:04:11.433 --> 00:04:12.587
Two hard limits.

69
00:04:12.587 --> 00:04:21.239
The keydir holds every key in RAM: fifty million keys of 40 bytes, plus the map's own bookkeeping, is several gigabytes.

70
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:26.852
And it's a hash map: it turns a key into a number that says where to look.

71
00:04:26.852 --> 00:04:30.586
That finds one key in one step, but keeps no order.

72
00:04:30.586 --> 00:04:35.858
So every payment on card 4242 between 9 and 10 means checking every key.

73
00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:40.105
Later lessons build indexes that keep their keys in order.

74
00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:46.144
So: write in pen, at the end, and keep a slip of where each key's newest value starts.

75
00:04:46.144 --> 00:04:47.871
Every write is one append.

76
00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:50.395
Every read is one lookup and one read.

77
00:04:50.867 --> 00:04:51.950
One quick check.

78
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.658
The power goes out and the slip is gone.

79
00:04:54.658 --> 00:04:58.111
The store reads the file from byte 0 to rebuild it.

80
00:04:58.111 --> 00:05:00.142
What's on the slip at the end?

81
00:05:00.142 --> 00:05:01.225
Pause and think.

82
00:05:04.433 --> 00:05:08.069
Only auth:2, with its value at 126, 8 bytes.

83
00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:12.532
The tombstone came last, so auth:1 stays off the slip.
